from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(9,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("measurement","Raju wanted to buy 3 kilograms of tomatoes and check the length of his notebook. \"Both are measurements,\" said his teacher.",
 ["Measurement means finding the size or magnitude of a quantity by comparing it with a standard called a unit. A physical quantity has a number and a unit, such as 3 kilograms. Quantities that cannot be written in terms of others are fundamental quantities, such as length, mass, time and temperature. Quantities made from them are derived quantities, such as area, volume and density.",
  "The SI system gives one standard unit to each quantity: the metre for length, the kilogram for mass, the second for time and the kelvin for temperature. Large and small values are written with prefixes such as kilo (1000) and milli, or in scientific notation like 9.46 x 10^15 m for a light year. For very large distances we use the astronomical unit, the light year and the parsec.",
  "To measure small lengths we use a vernier caliper and a screw gauge. The least count is the smallest length an instrument can measure. A screw gauge works with a pitch, the distance the screw moves in one full turn, and its least count is the pitch divided by the number of head-scale divisions. If the zero of the instrument is not at zero, we correct the reading using the zero error.",
  "Mass is the quantity of matter in a body and does not change from place to place. Weight is the force with which gravity pulls the body, and it is measured with a spring balance using Hooke's law. So a body keeps the same mass on the Moon but weighs less there."],
 ["fundamental quantity","derived quantity","SI unit","least count","screw gauge","mass and weight"],
 ("A screw gauge has pitch 1 mm and 100 head-scale divisions. Find its least count.",["Least count = pitch divided by number of head scale divisions.","1 mm divided by 100 = 0.01 mm.","So the least count is 0.01 mm."]),
 ("Mass and weight are the same thing.",["Mass is the amount of matter and stays the same everywhere.","Weight is the pull of gravity and changes from place to place."]),
 ["A quantity has a number and a unit.","Length, mass, time and temperature are fundamental.","Least count is the smallest reading an instrument can give.","Mass stays the same, weight changes with gravity."],
 ["m|Which is a fundamental quantity?|Length|Area|Volume|Area and volume are derived from length.","t|The SI unit of mass is the kilogram.|T|Yes.","m|The SI unit of temperature is|kelvin|metre|second|The kelvin is the SI unit of temperature.","t|Weight of a body stays the same on the Moon.|F|Gravity there is weaker.","m|An instrument for small lengths such as the thickness of a wire is a|screw gauge|spring balance|barometer|A screw gauge measures very small lengths.","s|What is the smallest length a scale can measure called?|least count|It is the least count.","m|A spring balance measures|weight|volume|time|It works by Hooke's law.","t|A light year is a unit of distance.|T|It is the distance light travels in one year."])
C("motion","Meena watched a train leave the station while her bus stood still. \"Which of us is moving?\" she wondered.",
 ["Motion is the change in the position of an object with respect to its surroundings. Distance is the total length of the path travelled, while displacement is the shortest straight-line change of position in a particular direction. Distance has only size, but displacement also has a direction.",
  "Speed is the distance covered in unit time. Velocity is the displacement in unit time, so it includes direction. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, and negative acceleration is called retardation or deceleration. A distance-time graph with a steeper slope shows a greater speed.",
  "For uniformly accelerated motion there are three equations: v = u + at, s = ut + (1/2)at^2 and v^2 = u^2 + 2as. Here u is the initial velocity, v the final velocity, a the acceleration, t the time and s the displacement. A freely falling body has the acceleration due to gravity.",
  "When a body moves in a circle at constant speed its direction keeps changing, so it keeps accelerating towards the centre. This is centripetal acceleration and the force causing it is the centripetal force. The apparent force that seems to act away from the centre is called centrifugal force."],
 ["distance","displacement","velocity","acceleration","equations of motion","centripetal force"],
 ("A car starts from rest and reaches 20 m/s in 5 s. Find its acceleration.",["Use a = (v - u) / t.","u = 0, v = 20 m/s and t = 5 s.","a = 20 / 5 = 4 m/s^2."]),
 ("Distance and displacement are always equal.",["Distance is the length of the path.","Displacement is the straight line between start and end, so it can be smaller."]),
 ["Speed = distance / time; velocity = displacement / time.","Acceleration is the change of velocity per second.","Three equations describe uniform acceleration.","Circular motion needs a centripetal force."],
 ["m|Displacement is|the shortest distance with direction|the total path|the speed|It is a vector.","t|Velocity includes direction.|T|Yes.","m|Negative acceleration is called|retardation|velocity|speed|It is also called deceleration.","t|A steeper distance-time graph shows a smaller speed.|F|It shows a greater speed.","m|The first equation of motion is|v = u + at|s = vt|F = ma|It links final velocity, initial velocity and time.","s|A body moving in a circle needs which force towards the centre?|centripetal force|Centripetal force acts towards the centre.","m|Unit of acceleration is|m/s^2|m/s|kg|Velocity change per second.","t|A car with changing direction at constant speed has no acceleration.|F|Changing direction means changing velocity."])
C("fluids","Asha pressed her palm and then a pin against a rubber balloon. \"The pin pops it,\" she said, \"but the palm does not!\"",
 ["The force acting perpendicular to a surface is called thrust, and the force per unit area is pressure. The unit of pressure is the pascal, which is one newton per square metre. The same thrust on a smaller area gives a larger pressure, which is why a pin pierces but a palm does not.",
  "Liquids and gases are called fluids. Pressure in a liquid increases with depth and with the density of the liquid. The layer of air around the Earth exerts atmospheric pressure, measured with a barometer, and one standard atmosphere is about 101.3 kilopascals.",
  "Pascal's law states that pressure applied to an enclosed liquid is passed on equally throughout the liquid. The hydraulic press and hydraulic lift use this law as force multipliers: a small force on a small piston gives a large force on a big piston.",
  "Density is mass per unit volume, and relative density is the density of a substance divided by the density of water. A body in a liquid feels an upward buoyant force. Archimedes' principle says this force equals the weight of the liquid displaced, so a body floats if its density is less than that of the liquid."],
 ["thrust and pressure","atmospheric pressure","Pascal's law","density","buoyancy","Archimedes' principle"],
 ("A force of 200 N acts on an area of 4 m^2. Find the pressure.",["Pressure = thrust / area.","200 N divided by 4 m^2.","Pressure = 50 Pa."]),
 ("A heavy body always sinks in water.",["Sinking depends on density, not weight alone.","A big steel ship floats because its average density is less than water's."]),
 ["Pressure = force / area.","Fluid pressure grows with depth.","Pascal's law explains hydraulic machines.","Archimedes' principle explains floating and sinking."],
 ["m|The unit of pressure is|pascal|newton|joule|1 Pa = 1 N per square metre.","t|A barometer measures atmospheric pressure.|T|Yes.","m|Pascal's law is used in a|hydraulic press|thermometer|compass|The press multiplies force.","t|Pressure in a liquid decreases as depth increases.|F|It increases with depth.","m|Relative density is the ratio of density of a substance to that of|water|air|iron|Water at 4 degrees is the reference.","s|What upward force acts on a body in a liquid?|buoyant force;buoyancy|The liquid pushes up.","m|A body floats when its density is|less than the liquid|more than the liquid|zero|It floats if lighter per volume.","t|Archimedes' principle compares buoyant force with weight of displaced liquid.|T|They are equal."])
